<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mieszko II Lambert</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 990–<a href="/years/1034">1034</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Mieszko II · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mieszko II Lambert was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from 1025 to 1031 and again ruled from 1032 until his death in 1034, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-i-the-brave">Bolesław I the Brave</a>. His reign saw attacks by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a>, revolts by his brothers, the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> and the royal crown, and the collapse of royal authority. After his death a pagan reaction and a Bohemian invasion devastated the country.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mieszko-ii-lambert.jpg" alt="Mieszko II Lambert" width="320" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darstellung_Mieszkos_und_Mathildes_von_Schwaben.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mieszko II was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-i-the-brave">Bolesław I the Brave</a> but the eldest born to his third wife, Emnilda of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a>. He could read and write and knew Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, which was unusual for the period. His father named him successor and used him in dealings with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. About 1013 he married Richeza, daughter of Count Palatine Ezzo of Lotharingia and niece of Emperor Otto III, and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> to rule, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wawel-hill">Wawel</a> as his residence.</p><p>In 1014 he was sent as an envoy to Duke Oldřich of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and was imprisoned there. He then led Polish troops in the war against Emperor Henry II that ended in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-bautzen">Peace of Bautzen</a> of 30 January 1018, on terms very favourable to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Bolesław died on 17 June 1025, and Mieszko was crowned king on Christmas Day by Archbishop Hipolit in Gniezno Cathedral. He inherited Greater Poland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a>, Silesia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Gdańsk Pomerania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-ruthenia">Red Ruthenia</a> and the territory of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. His half-brother Bezprym and his full brother Otto received nothing, since Bolesław did not wish to divide the kingdom, and according to some chroniclers Mieszko expelled them.</p><p>Opponents of Emperor Conrad II sought his help, and in 1028 Polish troops invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and devastated it. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> as an ally he invaded again in 1030, while the Hungarians briefly occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Yaroslav I the Wise conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-ruthenia">Red Ruthenia</a>, and in 1031 Conrad besieged Milsko, forcing Mieszko to give up lands. That year a Kievan army installed Bezprym, and Mieszko fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, where Oldřich imprisoned him again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bezprym lost his authority amid unrest and was murdered in 1032. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> on 7 July 1032 Conrad II divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> between Mieszko, Otto and a cousin named Dytryk. Otto was killed in 1033, Dytryk was probably expelled, and Mieszko reunited the country, though he still faced the nobility. He died suddenly on 10 or 11 May 1034, probably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a>, and was buried in the cathedral of St Peter and St Paul.</p><p>After his death peasants revolted in what is called the pagan reaction, and his son Casimir I the Restorer was expelled or driven out. Some modern historians think economic grievances, such as new taxes for the Church, counted for more than religion. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> invaded Silesia and Greater Poland in 1039, and the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>. He is called Mieszko II Gnuśny, the Lazy, which the sources attached to the way his reign ended.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mieszko II Lambert” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On Christmas Day 1025 <b>Mieszko II Lambert</b> was crowned king of Poland in the cathedral of Gniezno, where early kings of Poland were crowned.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mieszko II Lambert</b>, son of Bolesław I the Brave, was crowned King of Poland in Gniezno Cathedral.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-25">25 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Mieszko II</b> faced invasion and revolt, and in the 1030s a pagan uprising and a Bohemian raid devastated Greater Poland.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1025-1385">Kingdom of Poland (1025-1385)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.41,16.93&amp;year=1018" aria-label="Poznań on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4930 355 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Mieszko II Lambert"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5380 625h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5380 625h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=52.41,16.93&amp;year=1018">What was here in 1018? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
